Z||000000||Philphine||12-31-2000||08:32 PM||||<BR>been reading the old threads for cutting coils and i think i will. but, i'm hoping for a more definate answer for how much coil to cut off front and back for a 1.5-2" drop as even as possible. or higher in the rear if not exact. this is a ws6 susp.<P>also read the arguements pro and con so skip that part (i'm hard headed). i got lowering springs for my '87, but in keeping with the cheap idea i'm wanting to go the economical (barring other broken parts some think might happen) route. thanks for any help.  <P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||12-31-2000||08:35 PM||||I was going to ask the same question for my 88. I installed Fiero Store springs today and the front is way too low- it is sitting on the bump stops! How much should I cut off the factory springs to drop it about an inch?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||12-31-2000||10:02 PM||||The front is tough to calculate because the lower control arm acts as a lever on the spring. One thing is for sure, the amount of drop will be greater than the amount of compressed spring length that is removed. <P>The rear is a bit easier because the weight of the car acts directlty on the spring. For a 1" drop you need to remove 1" from the length of the compressed spring. <P>I hope that makes sense. It's been too long since I did mine, I don't remember how many coils or inches I removed.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||12-31-2000||11:38 PM||||This is unscientific and imprecise, but maybe it will help define the range.<P>I have an '85 SE 2.5 WS6.  I installed gas-pressurized Gabriel shocks and struts which raised the ride height.  Then I cut 1 coil from front springs and 1/2-coil from rear springs.  The resulting ride height is a little below stock, probably -1" or -1/2" in front and -1/2" in rear.  All measurements are estimates.<P>I don't remember which end of the front springs I cut.  It probably makes a difference.  In the rear, there's no choice.<P>The resulting ride is harsh but bearable.  If I were doing it again, I probably would get a set of softer junkyard springs, and cut them about the same to lower the car with a softer spring rate.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FieroGTRacingInc||12-31-2000||11:50 PM||||has anyone even tried those coil spring compressor's?||||reg||1
Z||000005||2xsess||01-01-2001||12:33 AM||||Cooter, I saw on someones site that they had the same problem with the Fiero Store springs. I'd call them before you go cutting anything.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||01-01-2001||10:00 AM||||I guess that I am disappointed in the whole spring swap that I wasted my time doing. The "lowering" springs are the same length as the factory springs, so the only way that I can see them lowering the car is by them being softer. I took the time to completly rebuild the suspension with new bushings, inner/outer tie rods, ball joints, and rack bushing. Now I will have to take it back apart to swap springs. Merry new year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
