Z||000012||sqoach||01-01-2001||02:24 PM||||a little o/t.<BR>so the fiero store's lower springs lower your car more than an inch as advertised? i'm thinking about those, but i don't want my car to be too low. thanks.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||01-01-2001||02:35 PM||||The Fiero store springs are Eibachs. I bought the 84-87 set (not sure about the 88 sets) Initially after swapping springs my car was 1.0" lower. After driving for a few months the cars was now 1.33" lower. It seem that the tighter suspension has to seat itself over time and you will drop another 1.4" to 1/3".||||reg||1
Z||000014||Greg Piet||01-01-2001||03:57 PM||||But which Eibachs are sold at the Fiero Store???<P>From what I gathered at <A HREF="http://www.eibach.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eibach.com</A>  <BR>Eibach has 3 different spring classes.<BR>1)Lowering kit<BR>2)pro kit<BR>3)road racing kit<P>I have not yet gotten my pro kit(ordered from carparts.com), but they are variable rate springs, so not only does the car drop, but as you increase load the springs are stiffer.  This will not only lower the car but improve handling more than just having a lower CG.  This should also remove the possibility of riding on your bump stops.  I do not see why anyone would want to just lower the car and keep the stock spring coefficient?<P>I would have gotten the road race kit, but I couldnt find any for the Fiero.  <P>As far as spring compressors go, I just used a set of $12 compressors on my buds Rx7 and it was cake.||||reg||1
